  "notes": "West leads the ♠K against 7♦. West's spade opening lead is won in dummy. South's only possible loser is in clubs. If hearts are 3-3, there will be no problem. If hearts do not break the squeeze chances are excellent since West is certain to hold the ♠Q.\n\nAfter drawing trump, South should cash the top hearts. If East has heart length, the contract is guaranteed on a double squeeze. On the last diamond West must abandon clubs to keep his ♠Q. Dummy discards the ♠J and East is squeezed in hearts and clubs.\n\nWhen West has length in hearts, there are no entries for a simple major suit squeeze. If West has the ♣Q or ♣J, however, a guard squeeze will develop. On the last diamond West is forced to bare his ♣J. Dummy discards from either major.\n\nSouth can now take 3 club tricks by dropping West's ♣J under the ♣A, and finessing against East's ♣Q. There is no ambiguity in the ending as West's last 2 cards are both known. If West had started with both the ♣Q and ♣J, the position would have been equally clear.\n\nThis form of guard squeeze matures with 1 loser. An opponent is squeezed in 3 suits. In 1 of the threat suits, the victim is protecting his partner against a finesse. There are no entries in either of the other threat suits, preventing a simple squeeze from maturing.",
  "commentary_paragraphs": [
    "West leads the ♠K against 7♦. West's spade opening lead is won in dummy. South's only possible loser is in clubs. If hearts are 3-3, there will be no problem. If hearts do not break the squeeze chances are excellent since West is certain to hold the ♠Q.",
    "After drawing trump, South should cash the top hearts. If East has heart length, the contract is guaranteed on a double squeeze. On the last diamond West must abandon clubs to keep his ♠Q. Dummy discards the ♠J and East is squeezed in hearts and clubs.",
